# Target C: sector peeling closes the one-deficit curved-wall population

Date: 2026-10-07. Authority: main
`4b0b982451effe3e8183edfce349ce4c25f9a3bb`, state
`2026-10-07.audit.74`. **Proved candidate awaiting independent audit.**
This report does not promote itself. C-II and Level I remain open.

## 1. Quantified statement and implication

For every fixed integer M>=4, the specified six-direction curved unit
wall admits a strict N-site interior packet, N=3M^2-3M+1, clearing every
old wall vertex except v=(1,M-1), for every sufficiently small positive
curvature. Consequently an arbitrary canonical triangle-free old source
with z<=N-1 points strictly inside that actual wall is excluded.

The same statement holds for every fixed strictly decreasing symmetric
rate list a_0>...>a_(M-1), a_(M-1-h)=-a_h, with a profile-dependent small
curvature onset. Arithmetic rates a_h=M-1-2h give the original wall.

`sector peeling -> simultaneous strict opening of N-1 auxiliary sites
-> add one protected root -> N strict sites -> pay every old interior
label plus one wall label -> full-source contact loss`.

The old cloud is arbitrary. The vacancy below is a construction in the
new packet, never a structural assertion about the old source. This
removes the all-M one-deficit opening obligation left by the M=4,5
certificates. There is no uniform onset in M asserted here, no extraction
of the specified wall from a general source, and no finite exclusion of
z=N. Those are separate obligations.

## 2. Exact sorting geometry and its complete contact graph

Identify the plane with complex numbers. Put u=1, v_basis=exp(i*pi/3),
w=v_basis-u=exp(2i*pi/3), z_h=exp(i*a_h*theta), and
S_r=sum_(h=0)^(r-1) z_h. In the sector between u and v_basis set

`p(a,b)=S_a*u+(S_(a+b)-S_a)*v_basis`, a,b>=0, a+b<=M,

and rotate this definition through all six sectors. The definitions agree
on their common axes. Symmetry gives S_M real, so the radius-M sites are
the six-direction wall with sorted ascending edge rates on each side.
For each fixed profile and sufficiently small theta>0, its turns are
positive, including the six corner turns pi/3-(a_0-a_(M-1))*theta.
It is a strictly convex unit wall. All nonconsecutive wall distances
remain strictly above one by their flat limiting gaps.

For an offaxis site a,b>=1, r=a+b<M, the four exact contact neighbours,
with their outgoing vectors from p(a,b), are:

| neighbour | vector |
| --- | --- |
| (a-1,b+1), called F | z_(a-1)*w |
| (a+1,b-1) | -z_a*w |
| (a,b+1), called D | z_r*v_basis |
| (a,b-1) | -z_(r-1)*v_basis |

The other two flat nearest-neighbour vectors are
z_(a-1)*w-z_(r-1)*v_basis and z_r*v_basis-z_a*w. Their squared lengths
are respectively

`2-2*cos(pi/3+(a_(a-1)-a_(r-1))*theta)` and
`2-2*cos(pi/3+(a_a-a_r)*theta)`.

Both exceed one for sufficiently small positive theta because the two
rate differences are strictly positive. Every other pair has limiting
distance at least sqrt(3). Finitely many continuity checks therefore
prove the complete graph, not only a selected subgraph.

On the axis p(r,0), 1<=r<M, the complete five outgoing contact vectors
are

`-z_(r-1)*u`, `z_r*u`, `z_(r-1)*w`,
`z_r*v_basis`, `-z_0*v_basis`.

The corresponding neighbours are (r-1,0), (r+1,0), (r-1,1),
(r,1), and (r,-1). The missing sixth flat neighbour has vector
z_r*u-z_0*v_basis, whose squared length is
2-2*cos(pi/3+(a_0-a_r)*theta)>1. At the center there are six contacts
to the first axis sites. These identities and the flat strict gaps also
prove unit separation of the entire chart for small theta. All moving
sites are strictly inside the wall since they are strictly inside its
flat hexagon at theta=0.

Delete auxiliary h=(1,M-2). Keep every wall vertex fixed, even v for
the moment. Let R be the matrix of *all* actual unit gradients involving
the remaining N-1 interior sites. No wall-wall row is included, since
it has zero moving gradient and imposes no packet-opening constraint.

## 3. Local rule for nonnegative equilibrium

Consider a nonnegative linear dependence among the rows of R. At each
moving site, the weighted sum of its outgoing unit vectors is zero.
An incident edge is called zero when its weight is zero; all edges to
the omitted h are absent and hence zero.

At any offaxis moving site, **if F or D is zero, every incident weight
is zero**. Indeed if F is removed, the angular gap from D to the inward
diagonal is pi+(a_(r-1)-a_r)*theta>pi. The remaining vectors lie in
an open half-plane. If D is removed, the angular gap from the backwards
ring vector to F is pi+(a_(a-1)-a_a)*theta>pi, with the inward diagonal
in the complementary arc of length below pi. Again all surviving
vectors lie in an open half-plane. An exact positive functional on
these vectors forbids a nonzero nonnegative equilibrium. All small
angle qualifications here are finite and valid simultaneously for
each fixed profile.

## 4. The vacancy empties a sector and then the complete graph

First every offaxis moving site in sector 0 is zero. In (a,b)
coordinates there is a directed path using F and D to h: use a-1
F steps and M-1-(a+b) D steps. It remains offaxis and inside radius
M-1 until ending at h. Work backwards from the absent endpoint using
the local rule. Thus every edge incident to every offaxis site of this
sector has weight zero.

Now empty the u-axis. For 2<=r<=M-2, the edges with vectors
z_(r-1)*w and z_r*v_basis end at offaxis moving sites in the empty
sector and are zero. The remaining three possible vectors are

`z_r*u`, `-z_(r-1)*u`, `-z_0*v_basis`.

The angular gap from the first to the second is
pi+(a_(r-1)-a_r)*theta>pi; the third lies in the other arc for small
theta. All three lie in an open half-plane and have zero weights.
This range is nonempty precisely when M>=4, supplying the axis seed.

At r=1 the outward axis edge is now zero, as is the edge to (1,1).
The three possible remaining vectors -z_0*u, z_0*w, -z_0*v_basis
span only an angle 2*pi/3. Their weights vanish. At r=M-1 the inward
axis edge and z_(M-2)*w edge are zero. The three remaining possibilities
are

`z_(M-1)*u`, `z_(M-1)*v_basis`, `-z_0*v_basis`.

The gap from the second to the third is
pi+(a_0-a_(M-1))*theta>pi. They also lie in an open half-plane.
Thus the entire moving u-axis, including its wall-incident edges, is zero.

In the previous sector, repeated F steps take every offaxis moving site
to this now-zero axis on its own ring. Backwards local propagation empties
that sector. Rotate the axis argument and repeat around all six sectors.
All moving axes and offaxis sites become zero. The six center edges have
zero weights at their axis endpoints. Hence every row weight is zero.
No nonzero nonnegative dependence among the rows of R survives.

This proof fails to provide an axis seed for M=3. The associated exact
local-pruning regression is retained rather than making a universal
M>=3 assertion. Wall-wall stress is irrelevant to R and was never
mistaken for an opening obstruction.

## 5. Constructive strict opening and a finite endpoint

Gordan's finite alternative already gives d with R*d>0. There is also
a direct construction using the same half-plane elimination, which
does not infer a finite endpoint from a weak first-order direction.

At each elimination step, collect all still-present edges at a moving
site p_i. Choose a vector n_i whose dot product with each outgoing
vector on those edges is positive. Remove these edges. The preceding
proof supplies an order eliminating all edges. Assign
`d_(p_i)=-lambda_i*n_i`, with lambda_i>0 successively small. When an
edge to an earlier assigned site is considered, its opening margin was
strict with this site's velocity zero, and remains strict for sufficiently
small lambda_i. Edges to later sites or fixed walls open strictly by
the defining half-plane functional. Finiteness allows all choices, and
every actual contact has a strict final opening margin. Sites left with
no edge can have zero velocity.

At each fixed allowed theta, use `p -> p+epsilon*d`. For each active
unit pair its squared distance is

`1+2*epsilon*(R*d)_e+epsilon^2*|d_p-d_q|^2>1`.

Every inactive lower pair and every interior support-line constraint
has a strict margin at epsilon=0, so choosing a common sufficiently
small epsilon>0 retains them. This is a finite strict N-1-site packet
inside the wall, clearing all original wall vertices.

Add the profile-flexible inward root from Section 2 of
`target_c_one_deficit_M5_extension_and_profile_root_2026-10-07.md`:
put A=a_0, Delta=a_0-a_1 and

`root=p(1,M-1)+3*sin(Delta*theta/2)*J(exp(i*(a_0+a_1)*theta/2)*w)`.

The omitted target h is exactly its negative-margin interior blocker.
All remaining root-to-chart and root-to-retained-wall pairs are strict;
its wall-neighbour squared distances are 1+3*sin^2(Delta*theta/2).
Its remaining limiting unit neighbour has first margin
(sqrt(3)/2)*(2*A-3*Delta/2)>0. Every support-line first coefficient
is at least 3*Delta/2>0. These calculations explicitly include all
root neighbours and every support line. Make epsilon still smaller
to preserve these strict root margins while opening the other sites.
The result has exactly N strict interior sites, clearing all wall
vertices other than the single intended deletion v.

## 6. Full-source ledger and remaining branches

Let S be *all* old source points strictly inside the actual wall, including
contact isolates. If z=|S|<=N-1, delete exactly S and v, and insert
z+1 distinct sites from the N-site strict packet. Every deleted label
is paid. No assumption is made about its contacts, coordinates or
diameter incidence.

- Retained old-old constraints do not change.
- Every new-new lower pair and new-retained-wall lower pair is strict.
- The canonical one-vertex convex-wall shield proves strict lower
  clearance from every retained exterior source point, including isolates.
- Every new point lies strictly inside the old two-dimensional hull.
  Strict convexity of squared distance gives distance strictly below
  the actual old diameter D to every retained point; it gives the
  new-new upper constraints as well. Actual old diameter pairs remain
  unchanged or disappear. All inactive upper exceptions are covered.
- There are zero new contacts, hence no new unit triangle. The retained
  contact graph is induced from the old triangle-free graph. At least
  the two old wall contacts incident with v disappear.
- Cardinality is exactly unchanged. If diameter falls below D, global
  optimality is contradicted. Otherwise contact minimality is contradicted.
  A diameter-pair saving is unnecessary.

The canonical dependencies are C-ONE-VERTEX-CONVEX-WALL-SHIELD and
C-CONTACT-BUDGET-DIAMETER-EXCHANGE-ENDPOINT. Only the explicit sorting
geometry is used, not the unreviewed arbitrary-cloud sorting classification.
Together with the audited Oler population bound, the original fixed-M>=4
wall class now has only z=N surviving at the small-curvature scope.

Next obligations: exclude that maximum population at finite curvature;
obtain a suitable actual wall or a direct packet in an arbitrary canonical
macroscopic chamber; handle paired exterior caps and independent full-core
face-coupled finite moves. Fixed-profile onsets alone do not control a
growing-M source sequence. The terminal goal remains active.

## 7. Exact checks

`research/verify_target_c_one_deficit_sector_peeling_2026_10_07.py`
independently enumerates complete actual contact graphs at rational
positive curvatures, records an exact open-half-plane separator at every
step, and verifies that all moving-involving edges are removed. It also
constructs successively scaled exact opening velocities and checks every
actual unit gradient. Its output is a finite certificate; the all-M and
all-small-curvature claims are the analytic arguments above.

M=3 is a local-pruning regression, not a universal impossibility result.
M=4,5 Laurent and finite endpoint certificates remain independent checks,
and are not used to extrapolate this theorem.
The constructive M=6 field also passes the complete strict-root finite
endpoint verifier at t=1/10000 and epsilon=10^-100: 212 opening rows,
7280 new lower pairs, 7280 upper pairs and 3276 containment tests.
